What is the historical context and purpose of the Planning Commission in the country?
Based on the provided context, the Planning Commission in both India and Pakistan was established to undertake economic planning and development initiatives. In India, the Planning Commission was established on March 15, 1950, with Prime Minister Jawaharlal Nehru as the Chairman, with the primary purpose of carrying out rudimentary economic planning. In Pakistan, the Planning Commission was established in 1952, with the aim of formulating five-year plans for the national economy and promoting the growth of the public and state infrastructure.

The Planning Commission in both countries was established to address the economic development needs of their respective nations, with a focus on planning and policy development. The Commission in Pakistan has also been involved in research studies and initiatives to connect poor landless people with high-end markets, indicating a focus on poverty alleviation and economic empowerment.

Therefore, the historical context and purpose of the Planning Commission in both India and Pakistan is to serve as a central agency for economic planning and development, with a focus on promoting national economic growth and infrastructure development.
