Describe the structure and function of the pulmonary artery, including its origin and destination.
The pulmonary artery is an artery in the pulmonary circulation that carries deoxygenated blood from the right side of the heart to the lungs. It originates from the right ventricle as the main pulmonary artery, also known as the pulmonary trunk, and is approximately 5 cm in length and 3 cm in diameter. The main pulmonary artery then divides into the right and left main pulmonary arteries, which further branch into smaller arteries, including lobar, segmental, and subsegmental arteries, eventually forming intralobular arteries. The pulmonary arteries ultimately lead to the capillaries surrounding the pulmonary alveoli, where gas exchange occurs, allowing oxygen to enter the bloodstream and carbon dioxide to be removed.
