What is the history of daylight saving time (DST) implementation in the United States, including the first year it was introduced and any subsequent changes or repeals?
The first implementation of daylight saving time (DST) in the United States was in 1918, during World War I, when the Standard Time Act was passed, which established DST as a temporary measure to conserve energy. However, it was met with opposition and was repealed in 1919.

The next implementation of DST was in 1942, during World War II, when the U.S. government reinstated DST as a way to conserve energy. This time, it was made permanent in 1945, with the Uniform Time Act, which standardized DST across the country.

However, in 1966, the Uniform Time Act was amended to allow states to opt out of DST if they chose to do so. This led to some states adopting DST while others did not.

In 2005, the Energy Policy Act of 2005 extended DST by four weeks, starting in 2007. This change moved the start of DST to the second Sunday in March and the end of DST to the first Sunday in November.

Today, most states in the United States observe DST, but Hawaii and Arizona (except for the Navajo Nation, which does observe DST) are exempt.
