What is the history of daylight saving time in the United States, including its initial implementation and any subsequent changes or repeals?
Daylight saving time (DST) in the United States was initially implemented in 1918 with the Standard Time Act of March 19, 1918, also known as the Calder Act. However, it was repealed in 1919. DST was re-established nationally during World War II, starting on February 9, 1942, and lasted until September 30, 1945. After the war, its use varied among states, with some adopting it and others not. There was no federal law governing DST from 1945 to 1966, allowing localities to choose when it began and ended or drop it entirely. The Uniform Time Act of 1966 established the current system of DST, which is observed in most areas of the United States, with exceptions including Arizona (except for the Navajo Nation) and Hawaii, as well as several overseas territories.
