When did the forced relocation of the Cherokee Nation begin, and what were the key milestones and events that occurred during this period?
Based on the provided context, the forced relocation of the Cherokee Nation, also known as the Trail of Tears, began in 1836 and lasted until 1839. The key milestones and events that occurred during this period include:

*   The forced relocation of the Cherokee Nation from their lands in Georgia, South Carolina, North Carolina, Tennessee, Texas, and Alabama to the Indian Territory (present-day Oklahoma) in the Central United States.
*   The resultant deaths along the way and at the end of the movement of an estimated 4000 Cherokee.

These events are referred to in the Cherokee language as "Nu na da ul tsun yi" (the place where they cried) and "Tlo va sa" (our removal).
